WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.570
Our DNA contains the instructions for
making all of the proteins in our

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.580
bodies. Watch "What is a Protein?" to
learn about the different things

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.830
proteins can do. So how do we get from
DNA to proteins? This flow of

4
00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:20.590
information is known as the central
dogma of biology. A section of DNA that

5
00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:25.520
contains the instructions to make one
protein is called a gene. A complex

6
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.820
machine called the ribosome needs to
read the instructions and use them to

7
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.470
build the protein. But there is a
problem. The DNA is huge and stuck

8
00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:39.790
inside the nucleus of the cell, while
the ribosome is outside the nucleus.

9
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.220
Since the DNA can't leave the nucleus,
the cell makes a copy of just the

10
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.850
instructions for one protein. The
copying process is called transcription,

11
00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:54.750
and a molecule similar to DNA, called
RNA, is used to make the copy. This

12
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.950
piece of RNA is small enough to leave
the nucleus and bring the instructions

13
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:03.480
to the ribosome. The ribosome reads the
instructions and builds the protein in

14
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.960
a process called translation. So in the
central dogma of biology, we say that

15
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.360
information flows from DNA to RNA to
protein. To learn more about how

16
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.000
transcription and translation work,
click here.

